cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "qubo-rerank: fair, energy-aware recommendation list selection via QUBO"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Huzaifa
    family-names: Abdul Rehman
# TODO: add `repository-code:` once the repository is published. Left out rather than
# guessed, because a citation file pointing at a URL that does not exist is worse than
# one that omits it.
license: MIT
keywords:
  - recommender-systems
  - quantum-annealing
  - QUBO
  - fairness
  - green-computing
abstract: >-
  Selects a k-item recommendation list by minimising a QUBO over relevance,
  intra-list diversity and group exposure fairness, with exact-k enforced as a
  penalty term, and reports the energy cost of doing so.

  Two findings are documented. First, the textbook recipe -- penalty-encoded
  cardinality handed to a single-flip simulated annealer -- fails silently at
  realistic candidate-set sizes: two feasible k-sets are never adjacent under
  single-spin-flip moves, so the sampler returns lists that are always the right
  length and close to arbitrary among lists of that length, losing to a greedy MMR
  baseline on its own objective. Tabu search and a constraint-preserving swap
  annealer both recover roughly a factor of two.

  Second, the choice of the diversity and fairness weights decides the
  QUBO-versus-classical comparison by a larger margin than the methods differ from
  one another; a comparison run with the fairness term disabled reverses the
  conclusion.
